---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RankWhy from '../components/RankWhy.astro';
import SortConsole from '../components/SortConsole.astro';
import IntroVideo from '../components/IntroVideo.astro';
import { APP_STORE_URL } from '../consts';
import BookDemo from '../components/BookDemo.astro';
import { PLANS, REVERSE_TRIAL_DAYS, TEASER_BULLETS } from '../plans';
import { FAQS } from '../faqs';

const title = 'QSortby — Collection Sorting & Upsells for Shopify';
const description =
  'Auto-sort Shopify collections on a rule you write — then check the result: why each product ranks where it does, what the last run changed, and what it earned.';

// Teaser shows the whole four-tier LADDER, not two anchors — the point of the
// section is that each step adds one thing, which two cards can't show. The full
// list per plan is on /pricing.
//
// The count itself lives in plans.ts next to the lists it slices, because each
// `features` array is ORDERED so that its first TEASER_BULLETS entries are the
// teaser set (marked with a comment at the boundary). Keeping the number here
// let an inserted bullet change the home page without anyone touching this file.

const mk = '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"/></svg>';
const x = '<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M6 6l12 12M6 18L18 6"/></svg>';
---
<Base title={title} description={description} faq={FAQS}>
  <!-- ===================== HERO ===================== -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap herogrid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Built for Shopify</span>
        <h1>Sort your collections. See why. <em>Prove it worked.</em></h1>
        <p class="lead">
          QSortby re-ranks every collection by live demand on a rule <b>you</b> write — the signals and
          their weights, in plain words — and A/B-tests it against your current order. You preview the new
          order before it publishes, so nothing goes live behind your back. No theme code.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-cta">
          <a href={APP_STORE_URL} class="btn btn-accent btn-lg">Install free <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a>
          <BookDemo class="btn btn-line btn-lg">Book a demo</BookDemo>
        </div>
        <p class="hero-note">Free plan forever · {REVERSE_TRIAL_DAYS} days full access on install · No card · GDPR-ready</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-viz"><RankWhy /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== SCOPE STRIP =====================
       This replaced a five-chip trust bar that restated the eyebrow ("Built for
       Shopify") and the hero note ("Free plan, no card", "No theme code") a
       second time, and carried a bare "Real-time" claim. Three of five items
       were duplicates, so the strip cost a screenful and said nothing new.

       What sits here now is the first thing on the page a visitor can CHECK:
       four counts, each traceable to the app. Specific beats adjectival — "13
       sorting rules" survives scrutiny in a way "Real-time" does not.

       ── This is the slot for real numbers. ──
       The moment there is a store figure worth quoting (lift, orders ranked,
       revenue attributed), swap a cell for it: `.hero-meta` is built for
       label-over-number and takes 3–5 cells. A sourced number outranks every
       count below, so replace, don't append. Never put an unsourced figure
       here — the site already had to walk back a "+37% revenue" that was only
       ever an illustration. -->
  <section style="padding:0 0 .5rem">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="hero-meta wide rv">
        <div>
          <span class="k">Ways to sort</span>
          <span class="n">11<span class="u">+ per-visitor</span></span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="k">Ranking window</span>
          <span class="n">1h<span class="u">to 1 year</span></span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="k">Ranking signals</span>
          <span class="n">10<span class="u">you set the weights</span></span>
        </div>
        <div>
          <span class="k">Upsell surfaces</span>
          <span class="n">6<span class="u">cart to thank-you</span></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== INTRO VIDEO =====================
       High on the page — right after the trust bar, before the first wall of
       argument — because a visitor who would rather be shown than told should
       not have to hunt for it. The hero keeps its own trust bar directly beneath
       it; the video takes the next slot.

       Renders only when the asset exists (see public/video/README.md), so the
       page is complete with or without it. -->
  <IntroVideo duration="1:37" />

  <!-- ===================== IS THIS YOU =====================
       Recognition before argument. Everything below this point argues that
       QSortby is worth installing; nothing above it named the merchant's own
       problem in their own words, and the home page linked to /use-cases
       exactly nowhere — the page was reachable only from the nav.

       Five sentences, each a deep link into the situation it names. Kept to one
       low strip rather than a section: its whole job is the half-second where a
       merchant thinks "that is my store", and a taller block would delay the
       argument it is supposed to set up. -->
  <section class="sec" style="padding-bottom:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shead ctr rv" style="margin-bottom:1.75rem">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Is this you</span>
        <h2>Five things merchants say <em>before</em> they go looking.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="saidstrip rv">
        <a href="/use-cases#buried-launch"><q>The drop I launched last week is still buried behind last season.</q><span class="go">New arrivals &rarr;</span></a>
        <a href="/use-cases#out-of-stock"><q>By Friday half my front row is out of stock.</q><span class="go">Live stock &rarr;</span></a>
        <a href="/use-cases#too-many-skus"><q>I stopped merchandising when the catalog passed a thousand SKUs.</q><span class="go">Scale &rarr;</span></a>
        <a href="/use-cases#flat-grid"><q>Forty products look identical &mdash; position is the only thing deciding the sale.</q><span class="go">Position &rarr;</span></a>
        <a href="/use-cases#browse-no-buy"><q>People browse for ten minutes and leave without buying anything.</q><span class="go">Per shopper &rarr;</span></a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== WHY NOT THE BUILT-IN SORT ===================== -->
  <!-- This section exists to answer the objection that kills most installs:
       "Shopify already has a Best selling sort — why would I pay for this?"
       Keep it high on the page; it precedes every feature argument. -->
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shead ctr rv" style="margin-bottom:2.5rem">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Why not the built-in sort</span>
        <h2>Shopify can sort by best selling. It can't tell you <em>what that cost you.</em></h2>
        <p class="lead-c">Shopify's native sort is one fixed rule applied blindly to everyone, forever. That's the bar QSortby is measured against — so here it is, side by side.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="ps rv">
        <div class="ps-col bad">
          <span class="tag">Shopify “Best selling”</span>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={x} /><span>One sort key for the whole collection — no window you can tune</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={x} /><span>Sold-out products hold their position until you step in</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={x} /><span>Same order for every shopper, first visit or fiftieth</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={x} /><span>No margin, conversion-rate or stock-cover option</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={x} /><span>Products that belong together scatter across the grid</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={x} /><span>No before/after — you reorder and find out once it's live</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={x} /><span>No attributed revenue — you can't tell whether it worked</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="ps-col good">
          <span class="tag">QSortby</span>
          <ul>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={mk} /><span>A window <b>you</b> choose — an hour for trends, a year for stability, or a set month</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={mk} /><span>Sold-out demoted within minutes of hitting zero, promoted back on restock</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={mk} /><span>Optional per-shopper feed — each visitor gets their own order</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={mk} /><span>Eleven signals: velocity, revenue, margin, conversion, trend, selling-fast, reviews</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={mk} /><span>Pairing rules keep matching products side by side — shirt, trousers, then shoes</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={mk} /><span>Preview the exact new order side by side, approve it, then publish</span></li>
            <li><span class="mk" set:html={mk} /><span>Every sort A/B-tested with attributed revenue and a significance verdict</span></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== FEATURE SHOWCASE (interactive) =====================
       Replaces the old static feature-bento grid with one live, interactive
       console: pick a signal (or a shopper) and the storefront re-ranks itself —
       demonstrating auto-sort + real-time + inventory-aware + personalization in a
       single object. See src/components/SortConsole.astro. -->
  <section class="sec alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <SortConsole />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== SHOWS ITS WORK =====================
       Sits immediately after the console because the console raises the exact
       fear this answers: "so it rearranges my storefront on its own?"

       ── This is the differentiating section on the page. ──
       It was framed as "You stay in control" — a reassurance. That framing
       undersold it. Auto-sorting, sold-out demotion and rules on sales/margin/
       stock are the whole category now; a merchant comparing apps in the App
       Store sees the same bullet list on every listing. What none of them offer
       is the ability to CHECK the result — why a product landed where it did,
       what the last run changed, whether it ran at all this morning, and what
       the revenue figure actually means. Those four are the reason to choose
       this app, so they lead here rather than sitting in three separate places.

       Every claim here is a shipped, UNGATED capability, verified in the app
       repo: the per-signal breakdown on the product page (routes/app/products/
       $id.tsx, no plan guard), the before/after preview, manual approval,
       manual membership, sort snapshot + config history, the run/pixel/sync
       state on the app home, and last-touch attribution. Nothing may be added
       here unless a merchant can verify it inside the app on the Free plan. -->
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shead ctr rv" style="margin-bottom:2.5rem">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Nothing happens in the dark</span>
        <h2>Plenty of apps re-order a collection. <em>This one shows its work.</em></h2>
        <p class="lead-c">Auto-sorting and sold-out demotion are the whole category now, ours included. What is <em>not</em> the category is being able to check any of it. Every answer below is on screen inside the app.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="steps rv">
        <div class="step">
          <div class="sn">Why this order</div>
          <h3>The rule is yours to read</h3>
          <p>You pick the criteria and how much each one counts, in plain words, and you can change either. On any product, the Similar tab goes further and names the signal behind every match &mdash; same type, shared tags, similar price. You read the reasoning instead of taking a score on faith.</p>
          <span class="utag on">On every plan</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="sn">Before it publishes</div>
          <h3>See the exact new order</h3>
          <p>A side-by-side of what is live now against what the next run would produce &mdash; real product cards, not row numbers. Added, removed and reordered are counted for you.</p>
          <span class="utag on">On every plan</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="sn">Your call</div>
          <h3>Approve, then push</h3>
          <p>Switch auto-publish off and a new order waits for your approval instead of going live. Or keep the collection manual: you choose which products belong in it, QSortby only decides their order.</p>
          <span class="utag on">On every plan</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="sn">Right now</div>
          <h3>Is it still running?</h3>
          <p>The app home carries the last run alongside the state of the pixel and the catalogue sync. A sort that quietly stopped is the failure that costs the most and takes longest to notice &mdash; so the app tells you, rather than leaving you to spot it on your own storefront.</p>
          <span class="utag on">On every plan</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="sn">After the fact</div>
          <h3>Every change on record</h3>
          <p>Config history keeps every ranking change with its date, so &ldquo;which rule was live on the 12th, and what did that week earn&rdquo; is a question with an answer rather than a guess.</p>
          <span class="utag on">On every plan</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <div class="sn">Is the number real</div>
          <h3>Attribution that says what it is</h3>
          <p>Revenue is credited last-touch, and the app calls it <b>influenced</b> &mdash; never <em>caused</em>. A test result is <b>directional</b>, never <em>proven</em>, and the minimum sample per arm stays on screen whether or not you have reached it. You always know what the number is, and what it is not.</p>
          <span class="utag on">On every plan</span>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- The A/B report used to open a section of its own, under the heading
           "Every sort has to prove it made you money" — a second H2 arguing the
           same thing the six cards above argue, which is the "one section, one
           job" rule broken in the open. The panel is the EVIDENCE for the last
           card, so it now sits under it; the 161 words of lead that framed it
           were saying what the card already says. -->
      <p class="snote rv" style="margin-top:2.25rem;margin-bottom:1.75rem">
        Sold-out demotion is the one thing that never waits &mdash; a product that hits zero sinks within
        minutes, and climbs back to its earned position on restock.
      </p>

      <div class="ab-panel rv">
        <div class="ab-exp" aria-hidden="true">
          <div class="ab-col">
            <span class="ab-hd">A · Control</span>
            <span class="ab-bar b1"></span><span class="ab-bar b2"></span><span class="ab-bar b3"></span>
            <span class="ab-cvr">CVR 1.9%</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ab-vs">vs</div>
          <div class="ab-col ab-b">
            <span class="ab-hd">B · QSortby sort</span>
            <span class="ab-bar b1 win"></span><span class="ab-bar b2"></span><span class="ab-bar b3"></span>
            <span class="ab-cvr ab-up">CVR 2.6% <em>▲</em></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="ab-meterwrap" aria-hidden="true">
          <span class="ab-meter"><i></i></span>
          <span class="ab-verdict">96% significant — <b>B wins</b></span>
        </div>
        <p class="ab-cap">
          Illustration of the experiment report, not a result claim. Every sort can be A/B-tested against your
          current order; QSortby tracks clicks → add-to-cart → attributed revenue and calls significance, so the
          numbers you act on are your store's own.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="rv" style="text-align:center;margin-top:2rem">
        <a href="/how-it-works" class="btn btn-line">See exactly how it ranks <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a>
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== THE SAME RANKING, ELSEWHERE =====================
       Replaces two PEER sections — "Per-shopper feeds" (504 words, 1,711px, the
       heaviest block on the page) and "Upsell surfaces" (290 words). Together
       they held a third of the home page's words, and neither is why a merchant
       picks this app over the dozen beside it.

       The v14 design system decides this rather than taste: "a set is a modifier
       on a surface, not a feature of its own." A per-shopper feed and a cart
       offer are both modifiers on one decision — which product goes in which
       slot — so they belong UNDER that decision at a line each, with the long
       version on /how-it-works. The IntentRead diagram moved there with them,
       where it also gives that page the visual relief it was short of.

       Ordered by where a SHOPPER meets each one, never by which tier sells it. -->
  <section class="sec alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shead ctr rv" style="margin-bottom:2.25rem">
        <span class="eyebrow" style="justify-content:center"><span class="dot"></span> Everywhere else</span>
        <h2>The same ranking, <em>in the other slots.</em></h2>
        <p class="lead-c">A collection page is not the only place your store picks products for somebody. Each surface below is filled from the same live ranking, so none of them is a second list to keep up to date.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="slots rv">
        <div class="slot"><b>For you</b><span class="where">Collection page &amp; feed</span><p>Each visitor can get their own order of the same collection, built from what this visit is doing &mdash; no login, no purchase history.</p><span class="utag">Growth</span></div>
        <div class="slot"><b>Best sellers</b><span class="where">Any theme block</span><p>A block that ranks itself on live demand and stays ranked.</p><span class="utag on">Free</span></div>
        <div class="slot"><b>Cart drawer &amp; cart page</b><span class="where">Cart</span><p>What is selling fastest right now, minus whatever is already in the cart.</p><span class="utag on">Free</span></div>
        <div class="slot"><b>Free-shipping progress</b><span class="where">Cart</span><p>How far the cart is from your threshold, and a product that actually closes the gap.</p><span class="utag on">Free</span></div>
        <div class="slot"><b>Frequently bought together</b><span class="where">Product page</span><p>Real co-purchase pairs from your own orders &mdash; and labelled as padded when there are not enough yet.</p><span class="utag">Starter</span></div>
        <div class="slot"><b>Thank-you page</b><span class="where">After payment</span><p>The order is already placed, so an offer here cannot cost you the conversion.</p><span class="utag">Starter</span></div>
        <div class="slot"><b>Popup</b><span class="where">Any page</span><p>Fires on a moment you choose, behind AND/OR rules so it does not fire at everyone.</p><span class="utag">Pro</span></div>
        <div class="slot"><b>In-checkout</b><span class="where">Checkout</span><p>Runs before payment, so an accepted offer joins the same order. Shopify limits this surface to Plus stores.</p><span class="utag">Growth</span></div>
      </div>

      <p class="snote rv" style="margin-top:2rem">
        <a href="/how-it-works">How the per-shopper feed reads a session &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ===================== PRICING TEASER ===================== -->
  <section class="sec alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="shead ctr rv" style="margin-bottom:2.5rem">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Pricing</span>
        <h2>Start free. Upgrade when <em>your numbers</em> say so.</h2>
        <p class="lead-c">Every install opens with {REVERSE_TRIAL_DAYS} days of full access — then settles onto the Free plan and keeps running. The app is never locked or switched off. Each step up adds exactly one thing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="plans plans-4 rv">
        {PLANS.map((p) => (
          <div class={p.feat ? 'plan feat' : 'plan'}>
            {p.badge && <span class="badge">{p.badge}</span>}
            <span class="ptag">{p.name}</span>
            <div class="price"><b>{p.price}</b><span>/ month</span></div>
            <p class="pdesc">{p.desc}</p>
            <ul>
              {p.features.slice(0, TEASER_BULLETS).map((f) => <li><span set:html={mk} /> {f}</li>)}
            </ul>
            <a href={APP_STORE_URL} class={p.feat ? 'btn btn-accent w-full' : 'btn btn-line w-full'}>
              {p.price === '$0' ? 'Install free' : 'Start free'}
            </a>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
      <p class="snote" style="margin-top:1.75rem">
        <a href="/pricing" style="text-decoration:underline">Compare all four plans in full →</a>
        <span style="display:block;margin-top:.6rem">Billed via Shopify · cancel anytime</span>
      </p>

      <!-- "Live in three steps" was its own 468px section between the upsell
           surfaces and pricing — a whole screen for thirty-six words, and it sat
           where nobody was asking the question. Effort is the LAST objection, so
           it belongs here, right under the price, as a strip rather than a
           section. Same three steps, a tenth of the height. -->
      <div class="setup3 rv" aria-label="Setup in three steps">
        <div><span class="s3n">01</span><b>Install</b><span>Add QSortby from the App Store. No code, no card.</span></div>
        <div><span class="s3n">02</span><b>Switch it on</b><span>Flip the QSortby embed on in your theme editor. One toggle.</span></div>
        <div><span class="s3n">03</span><b>Pick a signal</b><span>Choose how each collection sorts, preview the before/after, done.</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== FAQ ===================== -->
  <section class="sec">
    <div class="narrow">
      <div class="shead ctr rv" style="margin-bottom:2.5rem">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Questions</span>
        <h2>Good to know</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="faq rv">
      {FAQS.map((f) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{f.q}<span class="pm"></span></summary>
          <div class="ans" set:html={f.a}></div>
        </details>
      ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== FINAL CTA ===================== -->
  <section class="sec" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta rv">
        <span class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Get started</span>
        <h2>Let your storefront optimize itself — and <em>prove it did.</em></h2>
        <p>Install free, run it on one collection, and read the attributed revenue in your own dashboard.</p>
        <div class="hero-cta" style="justify-content:center">
          <a href={APP_STORE_URL} class="btn btn-accent btn-lg">Install free <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></a>
          <BookDemo class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">Book a demo</BookDemo>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
